      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L09-ACTIVITY-009",
      "SourceSection": "9.3: Which is Greater?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What happens when we multiply a number by a negative number? If the original number was positive, the product is negative. But if the original number was negative, the product is positive. The sign of the new number is the opposite of the…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l09.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L09-ACTIVITY-010",
      "SourceSection": "9.3: Which is Greater?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Now let’s consider the polynomial functions \\(f(x)=x^2\\) and \\(g(x)=\\text-x^2\\). For any non-zero real number \\(x\\), the output of \\(f\\) is positive while the output of \\(g\\) is negative. The signs of all the output values for \\(g\\) are…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l09.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L09-ACTIVITY-011",
      "SourceSection": "9.3: Which is Greater?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "This is the effect of a negative leading coefficient: the end behavior of the polynomial is the opposite of what it would be if the leading coefficient were positive. For polynomials of odd degree, we can see that a negative leading…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l09.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L09-ACTIVITY-012",
      "SourceSection": "9.3: Which is Greater?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Here are the graphs of \\(y=\\text-(x-1)(2x+3)(x+4)\\), which has a leading term of \\(\\text-2x^3\\), and \\(y=(x-1)(2x+3)(x+4)\\), which has a leading term of \\(2x^3\\). They have the same zeros, but opposite end behavior, because they have…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l09.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
